.lua
book.chapter(38, "DSLtk.hpp: graphs, terms, and equality saturation")
.endlua
.sh 2 "DirectedGraph owns stable handles"
.pp
DirectedGraph is a directed multigraph supporting isolated nodes, parallel
edges, self-loops, and cycles. C++ graph objects have value semantics; D
graphs are reference types. Node and edge IDs belong to one graph and are
not recycled. Removal invalidates the removed handles, while mutations can
invalidate native references returned by accessors. Retain IDs across graph
changes rather than assuming a reference into internal storage remains valid.
.lua
book.code("cpp", [=[#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <cassert>
int main() {
    dsl::DirectedGraph<> graph;
    auto entry = graph.add_node("entry");
    auto exit = graph.add_node("exit");
    graph.add_edge(entry, exit, "next");
    auto path = graph.shortest_path(entry, exit);
    assert(path && path->size() == 2);
}]=], "A complete native graph client")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[auto graph = new DirectedGraph!();
auto entry = graph.add_node("entry");
auto exit = graph.add_node("exit");
graph.add_edge(entry, exit, "next");
assert(!graph.shortest_path(entry, exit).isNull);]=], "The corresponding D graph construction")
.endlua
.pp
Adjacency preserves insertion order, including redirected incoming edges.
Traversals visit reachable nodes once; topological sorting rejects cycles;
strong components partition the live graph. DOT rendering uses internal IDs
as identifiers and escaped payload labels. It generates text without
launching Graphviz. Graph objects are mutable and unsynchronized, so an
application sharing them between threads needs its own ownership discipline.
.sh 2 "First-order terms make variables explicit"
.pp
Term represents function symbols with ordered arguments or variables.
Unquoted question-mark atoms in S-expression notation denote variables;
quoted forms remain literal symbols. Matching binds pattern variables to
subject subterms, with repeated variables requiring structural equality.
Unification solves equations involving variables on both sides and performs
an occurs check to reject cyclic equations such as x equal to f of x.
.pp
Substitution is simultaneous: inserted bindings are not recursively substituted
again during the same application. Unification composes its bindings so one
simultaneous substitution makes both inputs equal. This precise contract
matters when chaining analyses. A substitution map is not merely a sequence
of textual replacements, and first-order term operations do not automatically
understand lexical binding, alpha-equivalence, or capture avoidance in a
higher-order language.
.sh 2 "Tree normalization reports its stopping condition"
.lua
book.code("cpp-fragment", [=[dsl::TermRewriteSystem rules;
rules.add_rule("(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)");
rules.add_rule("(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)");
auto report = rules.normalize(dsl::parse_term("(mul (add a 0) 1)"), 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 2);]=], "Bounded first-order normalization in C++")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[auto rules = new TermRewriteSystem;
rules.add_rule("(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)");
rules.add_rule("(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)");
auto report = rules.normalize(parse_term("(mul (add a 0) 1)"), 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 2);]=], "The corresponding D normalization")
.endlua
.pp
Rules are validated so right-side variables are bound on the left. The
system tries rules in insertion order with outermost or innermost traversal.
A report distinguishes normal form from step-limit exhaustion. Reaching a
budget does not prove normalization, and identity rules still count as
applications. A zero budget can inspect reducibility without changing the
term. Termination and confluence are not inferred from the rule syntax.
.sh 2 "Shared graph reduction is a different operation"
.pp
TermGraph stores finite acyclic terms with shared subexpressions. Reducing
a shared redex redirects every incoming edge to its replacement, so one
reduction affects all occurrences sharing that node. Continue with the
returned root because rewriting a root can remove its old ID. Garbage is
retained until explicit collection with every root that must remain alive.
Unfolding a highly shared graph into a tree can expand its size dramatically.
.pp
The general DirectedGraph permits cycles, while TermGraph's construction and
reduction contracts require acyclic finite terms. Do not transfer assumptions
between them merely because both use node IDs. A step budget limits rule
applications, not memory usage or recursion depth. The difference between
tree rewriting and graph reduction is observable in work counts and aliasing,
even when both eventually render the same unshared expression.
.sh 2 "Equality saturation retains alternatives"
.pp
EGraph stores hash-consed e-nodes in union-find equivalence classes. Unite
asserts equality; rebuild restores congruence, so equal children imply equal
parent applications. Saturation matches rules and adds equivalent forms
rather than destructively selecting one rewrite path. Extraction then
chooses a finite representative under the supplied implementation's cost
policy. This separates discovering equalities from choosing an output.
.lua
book.code("cpp", [=[#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <cassert>
int main() {
    dsl::EGraph graph;
    auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
    auto rules = dsl::sexpr_rules(R"(
        (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
        (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
    )");
    auto report = graph.saturate(rules);
    assert(report.saturated());
    assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
}]=], "A complete equality-saturation example")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[auto graph = new EGraph;
auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
auto rules = sexpr_rules(`
    (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
    (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
`);
assert(graph.saturate(rules).saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");]=], "The corresponding D saturation")
.endlua
.pp
Saturation limits bound iterations, nodes, and applications, with reports
distinguishing saturation from each limit. Bounded partial results remain
useful but are not proof that every applicable equality was discovered.
Rules still require semantic justification. An e-graph can efficiently
represent invalid equalities just as readily as valid ones if the client
supplies unsound arithmetic or effect-reordering rules.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Repeated variables have different equality criteria in these systems.
First-order matching compares structural subterms; e-matching compares
equivalence classes, which may contain several different-looking expressions.
That difference is the reason equality saturation can discover matches that
a purely textual rewrite misses. Keep the criterion explicit when moving
a rule or a test between the two APIs.
.pp
Read DirectedGraph, Term, TermRewriteSystem, TermGraph, and EGraph in both
toolkit implementations, plus the graph/term regression tests. Compare
normalizing two duplicated subterms as a tree with reducing one shared
subterm as a graph. Then run an e-graph with a deliberately small budget
and inspect its stop report. Explain why valid partial output, normal form,
and saturation are three different claims.
